.TH GXWI-SERVICES-MANAGER 1 2026-10-19 "Peios gxwi-services-manager 0.1.0"
.SH NAME
gxwi-services-manager \- start, stop and look after the services on this machine, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-services-manager
.br
.B gxwi-services-manager \-\-definition
.I SERVICE
.br
.B gxwi-services-manager \-\-new
.SH DESCRIPTION
Services Manager lists every service defined on this machine, what each is doing, and when its next timer runs.
Find a service narrows the list by name or description.
The service picked is shown in full beside it: its state and why, its process, the account it runs as, how long it has run, what it has reported, and each timer, in local time.
Start, Stop, Restart, Reload, and Reset are below the details and in each row's context menu.
A command does not hold up the window: it is followed, and what came of it is said.
.PP
It asks peinit, on its control socket, as the person looking, and does nothing with more authority than they have.
A command is offered only where they hold the right it needs and it would do something in the service's state.
As shipped, Administrators can do anything with a service, and everyone signed in can see what it is doing.
Services they cannot see but whose definitions they can read are listed as Not yours to see.
If peinit does not let them in at all, the window says so, lists what definitions it can, and offers no command.
.PP
The state is asked again every two seconds; F5, or Refresh, asks at once.
The registry's \%Machine\eSystem\eServices key is watched, so a definition changed elsewhere shows straight away.
.PP
Edit definition... opens the service's definition in a window of its own, and New service... a new one: this program again, with the options below.
Every field is shown by group, checked as peinit checks it, and saved, with Save or Ctrl+S, in one transaction of only what changed.
Changing a definition needs the right to set values on the service's key, defining one the right to make keys under \%Machine\eSystem\eServices, and deleting one DELETE on its key; as shipped, only Administrators have them.
Without them, the definition is shown with every field fixed, and why.
.PP
Who can control it... and Who can change its definition... open the permissions editor on the service's ServiceSecurity and on its key; Default permissions... on the one every service without its own takes.
What the person cannot change is shown and not offered.
Logs... opens the service's records in Event Viewer.
.PP
Without a desktop to open on, it says so and ends: on a terminal, svctl does what this does.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-definition " SERVICE"
Open the definition of
.I SERVICE
alone, in a window of its own.
.TP
.B \-\-new
Open an empty definition, to define a new service.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Service definitions are stored under Machine\eSystem\eServices. peinit's registry manual owns the fields, validation, and application timing.
.SH SECURITY
Runtime control is authorized by peinit's service rights.
Definition edits use registry rights, separately from runtime control.
No request gains authority by being made through the desktop.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The application session ended normally.
.TP
.B 1
A connection, initialization, or application error prevented normal operation.
.TP
.B 64
Invalid command arguments.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GXWI_DISPLAY
The display connection supplied by the desktop session.
Launch through the desktop so inherited session descriptors and identity are available.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-services-manager.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Services Manager.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR svctl (8),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1).
Controlling services, Defining a service, and Who can manage a service, at learn.peios.org.
